  <p>Node.js 22 is scheduled to reach end of life on April 30, 2027. End of life does not make an application stop running on that date. It changes the risk equation: the line no longer receives the normal stream of maintenance and security fixes, and future ecosystem releases increasingly target supported runtimes. Teams that wait until an urgent vulnerability or platform image removal forces an upgrade lose the chance to test carefully.</p>
  <p>This checklist is designed for engineering leads who have more than one service and more than one deployment path. The most useful outcome is not “all manifests now say 24.” It is a verifiable inventory, a justified target, a list of known incompatibilities, and a staged release plan with a rollback decision made in advance.</p>

  <h2 id="timeline">Understand the support timeline</h2>
  <p>Node release lines move through Current, Active LTS, Maintenance LTS, and end of life. The exact dates are maintained by the Node.js project; check its <a href="https://github.com/nodejs/Release" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">release schedule</a> when budgeting a migration, because schedules can change. As of the planning dates referenced here, Node 22’s EOL is April 30, 2027. Node 24 is the LTS line suitable for many production upgrades, while Node 26 is the newer line to evaluate where the organization can accept its maturity and support phase.</p>
  <p>A target choice should weigh how long the service will remain on the selected line, the support status of deployment platforms, native dependency compatibility, and the organization’s release cadence. “Latest” and “least change” are both incomplete policies. A team shipping weekly may be comfortable validating Node 26 now; a regulated service with quarterly release windows may rationally select Node 24 LTS and schedule a second move later. Record the rationale instead of letting an image tag make the decision by accident.</p>
  <p>Separate the deadline into milestones. For example, inventory and ownership can finish well before the EOL date; representative services can enter a compatibility spike next; the first production canary should leave enough time to observe real traffic. Reserve a buffer for native rebuilds, vendor response times, and release freezes. A useful schedule has explicit “stop and reassess” gates, not just a date in a ticket.</p>

  <h2 id="choose-target">Choose a target for each service</h2>
  <p>Start with support policy, not a bulk edit. For each service, note whether its host supports the proposed Node line, whether its container base image is published for the target architecture, and whether every runtime used in local development and CI can install that version. A monorepo can have separate application and tooling runtimes; those constraints should be written down rather than collapsed into one number.</p>
  <p>Use explicit version declarations and make them agree. Here, the old project pins Node 22 in its developer version file and engines range while a container still tracks Node 20. The after example selects Node 24 consistently; adapt patch versions and image digests to your actual platform and update them through your normal image-refresh process.</p>
  <pre is:raw><code>// Before: package.json
{
  "engines": { "node": "22.x" }
}

# Before: .nvmrc
22

# Before: Dockerfile
FROM node:20-alpine</code></pre>
  <pre is:raw><code>// After: package.json
{
  "engines": { "node": "&gt;=24 &lt;25" }
}

# After: .nvmrc
24

# After: Dockerfile
FROM node:24-alpine</code></pre>
  <p>An engines range is a package-manager hint unless enforcement is configured. Keep it aligned with the actual runtime and add a CI assertion that fails when the selected major is outside the service’s supported range. Do not copy the same range into every package without considering tools, build images, and deployed functions separately.</p>

  <h2 id="checklist">The seven-point engineering audit</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Find every runtime selector.</strong> Search for <code>.nvmrc</code>, <code>.node-version</code>, Volta settings, <code>engines</code>, Dockerfiles, dev containers, CI matrices, release workflows, and platform-specific runtime declarations. Include scripts in sibling packages and infrastructure repositories.</li>
    <li><strong>Inventory the actual dependency graph.</strong> Read the committed lockfile, not only direct dependencies. Flag native addons and packages with platform-specific optional dependencies. Confirm support in upstream documentation or test the exact resolved version under the target runtime; a package name alone is not proof of compatibility.</li>
    <li><strong>Check build and deployment parity.</strong> Build in the same operating system, libc family, CPU architecture, and image family used in production. A macOS developer build does not validate Alpine Linux binaries. Rebuild native modules in the target image and do not copy ABI-specific artifacts between incompatible stages.</li>
    <li><strong>Exercise runtime APIs and cryptography.</strong> Inspect use of deprecated Node APIs, TLS configuration, custom certificate stores, OpenSSL provider behavior, and code that depends on undocumented runtime details. Run integration tests against the target major, including outbound TLS to representative endpoints.</li>
    <li><strong>Compare startup and operational signals.</strong> Measure cold start, memory, CPU, event-loop delay, shutdown behavior, and health-check readiness. Validate signals in a representative environment instead of inferring performance from a local benchmark.</li>
    <li><strong>Roll out with an explicit rollback.</strong> Keep the prior image or artifact available, define the metrics and error budget that trigger rollback, and verify database or queue changes are not coupled to a runtime rollback. A runtime downgrade cannot undo an incompatible persisted-data change.</li>
    <li><strong>Record evidence and ownership.</strong> Save the runtime matrix, tested lockfile, CI result, canary observations, and service owner in the change record. Assign an owner to every exception so “temporarily pinned” does not become an undocumented permanent state.</li>
  </ol>
  <p>For a fleet, prioritize by exposure and friction. Public-facing services, services with native modules, and workloads on old base images are often good early candidates because they expose unknowns. A low-risk internal worker can be a useful pilot if it exercises the same build chain. Track blockers in a shared table with the repository, runtime sources, native dependency notes, owner, proposed target, and next action.</p>

  <h2 id="rollout">Use a staged rollout, not a flag day</h2>
  <p>A reliable migration normally has four stages. First, make the target reproducible in local development and CI. Second, run the complete test suite and a production-like build using the target runtime. Third, release a canary or small traffic slice while monitoring application-specific metrics. Fourth, expand only when the evidence matches the baseline and the rollback path remains available.</p>
  <p>Keep this change isolated from broad dependency refreshes when feasible. Upgrading Node, a framework, and dozens of libraries together creates a large diff with ambiguous causes. If a security deadline requires several changes at once, split the work into independently reviewable commits and capture behavioral evidence at each boundary. Preserve the lockfile in every test artifact so later reruns can reconstruct the exact dependency graph.</p>
  <p>In CI, test both the source runtime and the packaged artifact. A container that builds successfully can still run against a different Node binary at deployment. Print <code>node --version</code> in the relevant jobs, inspect the final image’s runtime, and assert the expected major as part of release validation. If the platform chooses a version based on an environment setting or default, make that choice explicit and test the deployment configuration.</p>

  <h2 id="done">A practical definition of done</h2>
  <p>Call the migration complete only when all production paths have an owner-approved target; the runtime declarations agree; native modules are built and exercised on the target platform; integration and smoke checks pass; and the canary has met its observation window. Also verify that the previous artifact can still be rolled back, that security scanning understands the new base image, and that operations documentation reflects the selected runtime.</p>
  <p>Finally, keep a record of what was not tested. If a third-party integration has no staging environment, state that limitation and add a compensating production monitor. If a rarely used route was not exercised, say so plainly. Honest uncertainty is more useful than a green badge whose scope nobody understands.</p>
  <p>Neukee’s purpose is to make the pre-upgrade inventory and compatibility evidence easier to review: runtime drift, locked transitive packages, and source patterns all belong in the pull request conversation. It cannot replace a canary or a service owner’s judgment. Use the scanner to make the unknowns visible, then use your own deployment evidence to decide when the fleet is ready.</p>
</GuideLayout>
